{
  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 2 (sensors at x = ±0.05 m, y = 0.07 m; kp 3.0; kd 1.4; base_speed 1.2; mass 0.6 kg): the oval finished 20/20 (mean 1.92 s), the s-bend finished 18/20, and the slalom failed 20/20 at progress 0.26. On the slalom, max_error reached the 0.06 m off-track limit, RMS error was 0.023, steering oscillation was low (0.20), and each run had one line-loss event. Low jitter with a hard exit points to under-steering on rapid direction changes, not to oscillation. Two things limit steering on sharp alternating bends. First, kp 3.0 gives less steering authority than needed, so the robot drifts far before the turn command catches up. Second, the 12 mm sensors see the 25 mm tape only when it lies well inside their footprint, so the tape can go blank under the array on sharp bends, which causes the line-loss event. Changes: (1) kp 3.0 -> 3.8 to add steering authority for sharp direction changes; (2) kd 1.4 -> 1.6 to damp the faster steering response and keep the oval and s-bend stable; (3) sensor_size 0.012 -> 0.016 m so each footprint covers more tape at larger offsets and the line is less likely to go blank on bends; (4) base_speed 1.2 -> 1.1 to give the robot more time to turn on the slalom. The oval should still finish, with about 2.1 s expected, so the 3 s limit holds. Sensor layout, mass, body and wheel geometry, and ki (0.02) are unchanged to keep the change set attributable. Expected result: slalom progress rises above 0.5, s-bend completion stays at or above 18/20, and line_loss_events per run fall toward 0.5 or below. Hypotheses: (a) the slalom failure is steering authority, so kp 3.8 should lift slalom progress; (b) the larger sensor footprint reduces line-loss events on tight bends; (c) if the slalom still exits early with low oscillation, the next step is kp near 4.5 or a wider sensor spread, not more kd. Next measurements: per-track progress and completion, max_error and where it occurs, line_loss_events, steering_oscillation, completion_time and controller_errors, to see whether the slalom is limited by steering or by sensing.",
